  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        498.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In May 2004 Richard Thompson, a marine biologist at the University of Plymouth, and colleagues described an overlooked pollutant in the journal Science. Sifting through plankton samples kept since the 1960s, they found that microscopic plastic fragments and synthetic fibres had grown more abundant. They labelled the particles microplastics."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        410.96141732283473,
        461.8976377952756,
        490.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Since then the durable debris has proved ubiquitous, turning up on shorelines, in deep-sea sediment, in rainfall, and in Arctic ice. Regulation came before proof of harm to people: in December 2015 America passed a law banning plastic microbeads from scrubs and other cosmetics rinsed down drains."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        303.46141732283473,
        461.8976377952756,
        402.46141732283473
      ],
      "text": "The search turned inward. In March 2022 a small Dutch study found plastic in the blood of about three-quarters of 22 healthy donors. In March 2024 a study in the New England Journal of Medicine linked plastic in the fatty plaque of neck arteries to a higher risk of heart attack, stroke, or death. Its authors cautioned that correlation is not causation."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        156.36141732283474,
        461.8976377952756,
        294.96141732283473
      ],
      "text": "The most sensational figures are the shakiest. In June 2019 a report for WWF, a conservation group, reckoned people might swallow a credit card's worth of plastic a week; critics call that an overestimate. Two months later the World Health Organization was reassuring but tentative: on limited evidence, microplastics in drinking water posed no apparent risk at current levels. In February 2025 a study in Nature Medicine reported plastic in brain tissue in an amount popularised as a spoonful; others queried its methodology."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        88.46141732283473,
        461.8976377952756,
        147.86141732283474
      ],
      "text": "Detection has outrun understanding; human studies remain small or observational. Harm is plausible but unproven, and until the verdict is in, better measurement may guide policy more safely than panic."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        517.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "2004年5月，理查德·汤普森，一位海洋的生物学家，任职于普利茅斯大学，在《科学》杂志上，他和同事们描述了一种被忽视的污染物。细细筛查自20世纪60年代起保存下来的浮游生物样本，他们发现，极其微小的塑料碎片和合成纤维变得越来越多。他们把这些颗粒称为微塑料。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        429.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "此后，这些经久不烂的碎屑被证明无所不在，出现在海岸线上、深海沉积物里、雨水中，以及北极的冰层中。监管先于人体受害的证据出现：2015年12月，美国通过了一项法律，禁止把塑料微珠用在磨砂膏等化妆品里，这类产品用后会被冲进下水道。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        340.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        420.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "人们的搜寻随后转向了体内。2022年3月，一项小规模的荷兰研究发现，22名健康献血者中，约四分之三的人血液里有塑料。2024年3月，《新英格兰医学杂志》上的一项研究，将颈部动脉脂肪性斑块中的塑料，与心脏病发作、中风或死亡的更高风险联系了起来。该研究的作者警告称，相关性并不等于因果关系。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        212.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        332.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "最耸人听闻的数字，就是最不可靠的。2019年6月，一份和自然保护组织 WWF 有关的报告估算，人们每周可能吞下相当于一张信用卡分量的塑料；批评者称其高估了。两个月后，世界卫生组织的表态令人安心，但只是试探性的：基于有限的证据，饮用水中的微塑料，按照当前的含量，并未构成明显风险。2025年2月，《自然·医学》上的一项研究报告了脑组织中的塑料，其含量被大众传作“一勺”；其他人则质疑了其研究方法。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        143.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        203.66141732283472
      ],
      "text": "检测走在了认识前面；针对人体的研究仍然规模较小，或只是观察性的。说它有害合乎情理，却尚未证实；而在定论出来之前，依据更好的测量来指导政策，或许会更稳妥，胜过任由恐慌左右。"
    }
  ],
